      "paragraphs": {
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "index": 0,
            "blockIndex": 0,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Sounding objects can vibrate freely.\nAn external periodic force can drive them.\nIf the force's frequency is near their natural frequency, resonance can greatly boost the response."
          },
          {
            "index": 1,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Two identical tuning forks demonstrate **sound resonance**.\nSet them a short distance apart on wooden resonance boxes.\nLet the box openings face each other (Figure 83)."
          },
          {
            "index": 2,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "Tap one arm of one fork with a small hammer.\nThe other fork soon begins to sound as well.\nSound waves from the first apply a periodic force to it."
          },
          {
            "index": 3,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 2,
            "text": "Their matching natural frequencies allow a large response.\nThe response depends on the frequency difference.\nOutside the receiving fork's narrow resonance band, it is much weaker."
          },
          {
            "index": 4,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 3,
            "text": "Attach a light metal clip to either identical fork.\nThis shifts its natural frequency and can detune the pair.\nWith enough detuning, the response may become inaudible."
          },
          {
            "index": 5,
            "blockIndex": 4,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Tuning forks have little damping, so their resonance curve is sharp.\nThis is the behavior discussed in Section 14."
          }
        ],
